  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Locally heavy rainfall is possible today with showers\nand storms.\n\nA large mid level trough, centered over the Great Lakes, will\ndominate over the eastern CONUS today.. WPC surface analysis \nfrom 15Z shows a weak front draped across the SC Midlands. This \nfront will be the main forcing behind showers and storms this \nafternoon as it slowly moves towards the SC Lowcountry and SE \nGeorgia. While a strong storm cannot be ruled out, the main \nhazard this afternoon will be heavy rainfall as PWATs are \nanalyzed around 1.9 to 2.0\". The 12Z guidance depicts the bulk \nof the precipitation developing around 22/23Z this evening,\ngenerally along and east of I-95. The 12Z HREF shows \nprobabilities of 1 inch or greater east of I-95 around 50-70%.\nShower and storm activity is forecast to wind down by 9 PM. \n\nSunday Onward: The aforementioned upper trough will finally move\nfar enough east to usher in some drier air into the area by\nSunday into Monday. With drier air in place and the surface\nboundary pushed offshore, rain chances should remain low through\nMonday. However, moisture should begin to recover starting \nTuesday and into the middle of the week with a return to more \ntypical summertime pattern setting up.",
